response=r'''Uncertainty estimates are provided separately for training variability and test-case sampling uncertainty in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:interpretation_uncertainty}. We use the existing model-matched analyses of BiLSTM-Attention and BU-LSTM-Attention, each with the five fixed seeds 42, 123, 7, 2024 and 99. No new experiment, figure or supplementary table is needed for this response.

For grouped SHAP, Figure~\ref{manuscript-fig:r1m4_shap_comparison} and Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r1m4_shap_magnitude}--\ref{supp-stab:r1m4_rank_stability} distinguish five-seed means and SDs from pointwise 95\% calendar-day cluster-bootstrap intervals (2,000 replicates, resampling seed 42). The latter resample explained cases after averaging absolute attributions across the fixed fitted models, while retaining the same training background. For example, BiLSTM-Attention upper-tail mntpr mean absolute SHAP is 0.089130~mm\,h$^{-1}$, with seed SD 0.005174~mm\,h$^{-1}$ and a day-bootstrap interval of $[0.075460,0.102467]$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ (Table~\ref{supp-stab:r1m4_shap_magnitude}). These are attribution magnitudes: a positive magnitude or an interval above zero does not demonstrate independent predictive benefit or statistical significance of forecast improvement.

For ablation, Figure~\ref{manuscript-fig:r1m4_ablation_comparison} and Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r1m4_ablation_bilstm_attn}--\ref{supp-stab:r1m4_seed_pairs_bulstm_attn} give the five seed-paired differences $\Delta\mathrm{RMSE}=\mathrm{RMSE}_{\mathrm{removed}}-\mathrm{RMSE}_{\mathrm{full}}$, their mean and sample SD, pointwise 95\% Student-$t$ intervals ($df=4$), and paired moving-block bootstrap intervals (28 six-hourly samples per block, 10,000 replicates, resampling seed 42). The bootstrap uses the same blocks for full/reduced models and across seeds; each within-seed RMSE difference is calculated before averaging the differences. It is not computed from an ensemble-mean prediction. Removing sp from BiLSTM-Attention increases overall RMSE by 0.004022~mm\,h$^{-1}$ (seed SD 0.000606), with a seed-$t$ interval $[0.003270,0.004774]$ and block-bootstrap interval $[0.002527,0.005547]$~mm\,h$^{-1}$. All five seeds deteriorate (Table~\ref{supp-stab:r1m4_ablation_bilstm_attn}).

The negative findings are also retained. Removing mntpr from BU-LSTM-Attention reduces RMSE on the full, revised-wet ($y\geq0.1$~mm\,h$^{-1}$) and upper-tail subsets by paired mean differences of $-0.001901$, $-0.010509$ and $-0.020662$~mm\,h$^{-1}$, respectively (Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r1m4_ablation_bulstm_attn} and~\ref{supp-stab:r1m4_seed_pairs_bulstm_attn}). Each subset meets all three descriptive robustness criteria used here: improvement in all five seeds, a seed-$t$ interval excluding zero, and a paired block-bootstrap interval excluding zero. This is specific to the tested architecture, configurations and evaluation subsets; it does not show that mntpr is generally harmful or establish meteorological causality. In contrast, BU-LSTM-Attention sp and tcc removal increases full-test error in only four of five seeds: their block-bootstrap intervals are above zero but their seed-$t$ intervals include zero. They are not described as uniformly robust important predictors.

The limitations are explicit. SHAP bootstrap intervals condition on the fitted models and do not include training randomness; sensitivity to a different fixed training background was not evaluated. The temporal SHAP analysis is a separate finite-permutation approximation without its own confidence intervals, and is not substituted for grouped attribution uncertainty. Ablation seed intervals are based on only five fitted models. Training and case-sampling uncertainty have not been combined into a total interval. All intervals are pointwise and unadjusted for multiple comparisons; the three-way rule is not a multiplicity correction. The existing manuscript and figures are retained unchanged. Final page and line numbers remain pending full-manuscript assembly.'''
